text:
elena: Customer on Electron (Windows 11) reports stream stops mid-response and their JSON parser throws 'Unexpected end of JSON input'. They see final SSE event never arrives, connection shows 200 then connection-close. SDK v1.12.0 (node) + app uses our EventSource polyfill. Attaching minimal repro and logs.

sam: thanks elena — can you paste the minimal client snippet and server-side headers/logs?

elena: sure. repro steps: 1) start simple express proxy that forwards /v1/stream to our API with keep-alive. 2) use eventsource-polyfill in electron renderer. 3) request a streaming completion for long output (~8k chars). sometimes final JSON object is truncated mid-key.

elena: client snippet:```js
import EventSource from 'eventsource';
const es = new EventSource('https://proxy.local/v1/stream?model=test');
es.onmessage = (e) => { try { const obj = JSON.parse(e.data); console.log('chunk', obj); } catch (err) { console.error('parse error', err, 'raw=', e.data); } }
```

elena: server-side captured SSE frames (what proxy logged). note: frames include partial JSON with no \n

```
id: 1
data: {"text":"hello world \n\n"}
```
```
id: 2
data: {"text":"continues here","meta":{
```
```
id: 3
data: {"text":"final bits"}

```

But in failing runs we saw id:2 frame arrive split across two TCP segments and proxy wrote them out as two separate SSE events, leaving a final partial object that the client treats as complete event with incomplete JSON (no more data).

leo: I'm the customer — confirm we see this more in packaged electron builds, dev mode less frequent. the polyfill is eventsource from npm.

devin: interesting. sounds like our streaming runtime sometimes emits newlines within data fields and proxies that don't coalesce chunks can turn mid-object splits into separate SSE "data:" events. EventSource semantics: each "data:" block until blank line denotes one event; if a proxy flushes a TCP segment in the middle of an object but still within same SSE event, client will get incomplete event only if the proxy inserted a blank line. Are you seeing blank-line insertion?

elena: proxy is naive: it proxies chunks from upstream to downstream as they arrive, writing flushes. it doesn't join buffers to SSE event boundaries. stack: node http proxy using res.write(chunk); res.flush?.

sam: I think we've seen regressions where response chunking + certain proxies (express with compression off) cause our runtime to emit partial data+

maya: quick sanity: our runtime does not intentionally split SSE events with blank-line boundaries; we stream a single event with multi-line data sometimes. But there was a change in 1.14.0 where we added periodic small flushes to reduce latency (microflush). Could microflush cause mid-event blank-lines? It shouldn't insert blank lines, only flush the buffer.

elena: logs from the proxy show it calls res.write on each 'data' chunk and then sometimes writes '\n' between chunks because of a simple formatting helper. that helper may be adding blank lines when chunk ends with newline.

leo: repro log from electron: parse error Unexpected end of JSON input raw= "{\"text\":\"continues here\",\"meta\":{\n"  (note trailing open brace)

devin: OK so the specific failure mode: proxy is turning a single SSE event into multiple events by inserting a blank line between TCP-fragmented pieces. client sees an event whose data is incomplete JSON. two approaches: 1) fix the proxy to avoid inserting blank line mid-event (coalesce until full SSE event), 2) make client more tolerant and buffer multiple events when JSON.parse fails and treat them as a continuation (risky), 3) change runtime to ensure we never emit newline sequences that could be misinterpreted when proxied (e.g., base64-encode payload, or send length-prefixed frames).

sam: which path does support prefer for customer? leo wants a quick workaround and a long-term fix.

leo: workaround = update proxy logic in our app maybe fastest. would like to know if SDK can robustly handle this too — our desktop app can push a patch quickly but it's friction for customers.

maya: short-term: we'll add a support doc recommending proxies avoid splitting SSE events and show a safe proxy pattern. medium-term: propose SDK change for Node/eventsource-polyfill: if JSON.parse fails on an event and event.data does not end with '\n' or '}' we can hold that event as "maybe partial" for 100ms and append the next event if it arrives quickly. But that breaks strict SSE semantics.

devin: alternative: in runtime, add an optional flag add_frame_boundaries=true that will emit an SSE comment line with event boundary marker (e.g., ":frame\n") only at safe boundaries so proxies won't accidentally create blank lines mid-event. That would be backward compatible. We can gate behind a model param for now.

sam: can we repro reliably? elena, can you produce a tiny script that simulates the proxy inserting blank lines when a chunk boundary occurs? we can run our SDK e2e.

elena: yes, created quick proxy:```js
const http = require('http');
const httpProxy = require('http-proxy');
const proxy = httpProxy.createProxyServer({});
http.createServer((req,res)=>{
  proxy.web(req,res,{target:'https://api.redwood.test'},(e)=>res.end());
}).listen(8080);

// monkey patch proxy to insert blank line randomly on chunk boundaries
proxy.on('proxyRes', (proxyRes, req, res) => {
  proxyRes.on('data', chunk => {
    // occasionally insert extra '\n' between chunks to simulate buggy middleware
    if (Math.random()<0.2) res.write('\n');
    res.write(chunk);
  });
});
```

elena: with that proxy we can trigger parse failures in ~20% of runs in our packaged electron.

sam: good repro. next steps: 1) publish support doc + recommended proxy snippet, 2) add SDK guard (best-effort buffer-on-parse-error) in a patch in v1.12.x branch, 3) runtime feature flag for explicit frame markers for customers who opt in.

devin: I'll open runtime ticket for frame-marker opt-in and ping maya for prioritization. also will add unit test to ensure microflush doesn't add blank lines.

infra-bot: deploy scheduled: runtime hotfix will not be auto-deployed; requires opt-in parameter.

sam: elena/leo — can you validate the support doc draft and the client patch once we have it?

leo: yes we'll test and follow up. thanks all. :pray:
